WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:05.242
Stage fourteen: the night, the rock and the roadbook.

2
00:00:06.092 --> 00:00:12.246
Camille dreams of an ultra next year: a four p.m. start, on the night the clocks go back.

3
00:00:12.346 --> 00:00:23.564
The plan computes the sun locally, with no network: headlamp from six fifty-six p.m. to four oh eight a.m., and five percent more time in full darkness, more on descents.

4
00:00:23.664 --> 00:00:26.962
The clock stays right across the time change.

5
00:00:27.812 --> 00:00:38.310
Elevation gain feeds almost everything. Optionally, the plan corrects it with a digital terrain model: IGN in France, Copernicus elsewhere.

6
00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:46.412
The report always shows both values. Only thinned coordinates leave your machine, and your own sessions stay home.

7
00:00:47.262 --> 00:00:54.928
An alpine trail isn't run like a fire road. From OpenStreetMap, each section gets a technicality coefficient.

8
00:00:55.028 --> 00:01:03.126
But relative to your usual terrain: only the gap with what you already run is counted. Here, a maximum of one point one nine.

9
00:01:03.976 --> 00:01:10.178
It all ends up in the roadbook, one sheet per scenario, with the profile and the night hatched.

10
00:01:10.278 --> 00:01:19.240
Then every section: passage time on the local clock, cutoff and its margin, “tight” spelled out, and what to take at the aid station.

11
00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:28.092
Mandatory gear becomes a checklist, checked against your inventory: the jacket is missing, the blanket never used in training.

12
00:01:28.192 --> 00:01:38.210
All of it prints on A4, black and white, or saves as a PDF. Missing data is said missing, never invented.

13
00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:41.518
Three pages to keep handy.

14
00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:53.210
To go further: the “Race strategist” page of the documentation.
